      "input_tokens": 1937,
      "output_tokens": 275,
      "response": "Sure. Here are two practical habits for legible field notes that another researcher can easily revisit:\n\n1. **Write in clear, consistent handwriting or type with a standard font**  \n   Use a font like Arial, Calibri, or Helvetica\u2014simple, sans-serif, and easy to read. If handwriting, write in a single, consistent style (e.g., cursive or block letters) so that entries are uniform and not confusing. Avoid messy or overly decorative scripts. This helps someone else quickly scan and understand the content without needing to guess what was meant.\n\n2. **Include a brief, structured header for each entry**  \n   At the top of each page or section, write:  \n   - Date and time (e.g., October 27, 2026, 14:30)  \n   - Location (e.g., Field site B, near the river bend)  \n   - Brief context (e.g., \"Observation of bird behavior during morning light\")  \n   This gives a clear frame for what was recorded and when, making it easier to locate and interpret later.\n\nThese habits don\u2019t require tools or extra time\u2014they just take a few seconds to add each time you write. And because they\u2019re simple and visible, they help anyone revisiting the notes understand the scene, timing, and purpose without confusion.\n\n*[Source: model knowledge (not checked against a source)]*\n*[Answered by Ollama \u00b7 qwen3:4b-instruct]*",
